course: dictionary
lesson: 10044
title: Terribly: three meanings, one word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word terribly can mean three different things, so you must read the words around it to know which one fits. «I'm terribly sorry I am late.» Here terribly means very or extremely. People say it a lot in polite British speech, and it makes the sorry stronger.
explain: «He sang terribly, so everyone covered their ears.» Here terribly means very badly, in an awful way. The clue is the covered ears: the singing was bad. Do not read it as "very" here, because «he sang very, so everyone covered their ears» makes no sense.
explain: «She was terribly hurt when she fell off the bike.» Here terribly means in a way that causes great suffering. The clue is hurt, which shows pain. Always test your meaning: put it back into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: Terribly is an adverb, so it tells you more about a verb, an adjective, or another adverb.
fact: «Terribly» can mean very or extremely, as in «terribly kind» or «terribly sorry».
fact: «Terribly» can mean very badly, as in «he sang terribly».
fact: «Terribly» can mean in a way that causes great suffering, as in «she was terribly hurt».
fact: Terribly sorry and terribly important are common phrases, and the first meaning is the one you will hear most often.
exercise: «I'm terribly sorry I am late.» In this sentence, what does terribly mean? | *Very, or extremely | Slowly | Very badly, in an awful way | In a way that causes great suffering
exercise: «He sang terribly, so everyone covered their ears.» What does terribly mean here? | Beautifully | Very loudly | *Very badly, in an awful way | Very happily
exercise: «She was terribly hurt when she fell off the bike.» What does terribly suggest? | *Great suffering, badly hurt | Very happy and excited | Quickly | Only a little bit
test: «The rain was terribly heavy, and the match had to stop.» What does terribly mean here? | *Very, or extremely | Slowly | Badly, in an awful way | Gently
test: «Though Toad grumbles terribly, the barge-woman is paid for her horse.» What does terribly mean here? | Very quietly and politely | Not at all | Like a good singer | *Very much, in a bad-tempered way
exam: «The lost puppy was terribly cold and frightened.» What does terribly mean here? | Very politely | Happily and easily | Only a little bit | *In a way that causes great suffering
